Based on my analysis, the `GlobalSearchView` follows the established object-level authorization pattern. For objects that support object-level permissions (Document, Tag, Correspondent, DocumentType, StoragePath), the code uses `get_objects_for_user_owner_aware`, which restricts results to objects the user owns, owns no owner (shared), or has explicit perms on. For each such type, a global `has_perm` gate is also applied.

For types that are inherently global (Users, Groups, MailRule, MailAccount, Workflow, CustomField), the code queries the model directly but gates them with a global `has_perm` check, consistent with the policy that "global permissions determine if a user can view any" of those types and that Workflows "are global... all users who can access workflows have access to the same set." SavedViews are restricted to `owner=request.user`.

I could not confirm a violation with cited evidence — no policy rule is explicitly broken by the changed lines, and the authorization checks (IsAuthenticated + per-type `has_perm` + object-level filtering) are present and consistent with the documented model.

